### PRIORITY 1: Proactive Vaccination and Immunisation Uptake Optimisation (Influenza, COVID-19, RSV, Pneumococcal)

**Definition:** Structured, population-level programmes to maximise uptake of respiratory vaccines in eligible groups, with targeted outreach to under-vaccinated populations.

**GP Perspective Rationale:** Each winter I see patients who were eligible for flu vaccine and did not receive it. The downstream cost — in hospitalisations, GP consultations, critical care admissions — is substantial. Vaccine hesitancy has increased post-COVID. Equity gaps are marked.

| Criterion | Score | Rationale |
|-----------|-------|-----------|
| Magnitude of winter problem | 5 | Influenza alone causes 11,000–33,000 deaths annually in England in high-burden winters (PHE estimates, pre-COVID data). COVID and RSV compound this. Vaccine-preventable disease directly drives winter surge. |
| Strength of evidence | 5 | Cochrane systematic reviews confirm influenza vaccine effectiveness in reducing hospitalisation in older adults and those with chronic disease. RSV vaccine evidence now emerging for adults ≥60 (NEJM 2023 data for RSVPreF3 vaccine). |
| Cost-effectiveness | 5 | Influenza vaccination is one of the most cost-effective public health interventions documented. NHS Economic Evaluation Programme data consistently support this. |
| NHS/Life Sciences alignment | 5 | Core NHS England CQRS/QOF indicator. Aligns with UKHSA immunisation strategy. UK Life Sciences sector directly involved in vaccine development and manufacture. |
| Likely impact timeline | 5 | Short-term impact within single season of improved uptake. Near-immediate benefit. |
| Reducing health inequalities | 4 | Significant equity gaps documented in BAME communities, deprived areas, and homeless populations. Targeted outreach can address this, though structural barriers persist. |

**Composite Score Calculation:**
