### PRIORITY 3: Anticipatory/Proactive Care Planning for High-Risk Patients Before Winter

**Definition:** Systematic identification of patients at high risk of winter deterioration (frail elderly, COPD, heart failure, severe asthma, immunocompromised) and delivery of structured proactive care plans, rescue medication provision, and self-management support before the winter season.

**GP Perspective Rationale:** I know, looking at my list, which patients will end up admitted in January. The challenge is finding the time in September and October to intervene proactively. This is a workflow and resource challenge, but the evidence for anticipatory care is strong.

| Criterion | Score | Rationale |
|-----------|-------|-----------|
| Magnitude of winter problem | 5 | A minority of patients (approximately 5% of a GP list) account for the majority of winter hospital admissions. Predictive risk stratification is well-established. |
| Strength of evidence | 4 | Strong observational evidence and service evaluation data. RCT evidence for specific anticipatory care components (e.g., COPD rescue packs, heart failure monitoring) is good. Whole-programme RCT evidence is mixed (Evercare programme evaluation showed limited impact on emergency admissions in UK, though methodology debated). |
| Cost-effectiveness | 4 | Individual components (COPD bundles, heart failure monitoring) are cost-effective. Whole-programme cost-effectiveness is harder to demonstrate, partly due to measurement challenges. |
| NHS/Life Sciences alignment | 5 | Core to NHS Long Term Plan. PCN DES requirements include personalised care and structured reviews. NHS England investment in proactive care models. |
| Likely impact timeline | 4 | Medium-term at programme level (3–5 years for systematic implementation). Individual patient benefit can be rapid. |
| Reducing health inequalities | 4 | Deprivation strongly correlates with emergency admissions. Proactive care targeting deprived populations has equity potential. Requires culturally sensitive delivery. |

