## PRIORITY 11: Enhanced Care in Care Homes

### Intervention 11.1: Weekly GP/APN Clinical Rounds in Care Homes with Standardised Sick-Day Protocols

**Implementation Level:** Clinical/Organisational

**Mechanism:** Named GP or Advanced Practice Nurse (APN) conducting weekly structured ward rounds in care homes within the PCN, using standardised clinical assessment protocols, advanced care planning reviews, and sick-day rules (escalation criteria for when to call for acute review versus 999) to reduce inappropriate emergency transfers.

**Resources Required:**
- *Medium:* APN or GP time (approximately 2–3 hours per care home per week). Structured clinical documentation tools.

**Implementation Requirements:** PCN EHCH DES enrolment, named GP/APN for each care home, digital clinical records for care homes (ideally GP system access for care home staff), advance care planning documentation.

**Strength of Evidence:**
- *Type A:* NHS England EHCH national evaluation (Sherlaw-Johnson et al., HSDR report, 2021) — EHCH model associated with reduced emergency hospital admissions and improved quality of care. Proactive GP care in nursing homes: Graverholt et al. systematic review (BMC Health Services Research, 2014).
- *Type B:* NHS England EHCH Framework and PCN DES guidance.

**Feasibility:** PCN DES EHCH provides structure. Quality implementation within **1–3 years.**

**Barriers:** GP capacity for additional care home time; digital records access for care home staff; care home staff engagement and training; variable care home management quality.

**Metrics:** Emergency admission rates from care homes per 1000 bed-days; 999 call rates from care homes; care home staff satisfaction; advance care planning documentation rates.

**Evidence Classification:** MODERATE EVIDENCE (service evaluation data; RCT evidence limited)

