# SECTION G: CROSS-CUTTING INTERVENTION STRATEGIES

## Cross-Cutting Strategy 1: The PCN as the Delivery Vehicle
Nearly every top-10 intervention is best delivered at PCN level, not individual practice level. PCNs provide the scale, MDT infrastructure, and ARRS workforce to deliver proactive care, social prescribing, clinical pharmacists, mental health practitioners, and care coordinators. **Policy implication:** Investing in PCN maturity and governance is the single most cross-cutting enabling intervention.

## Cross-Cutting Strategy 2: Autumn Pre-Winter Preparation Window
Multiple interventions (COPD reviews, frailty assessments, vaccination, advance care planning, proactive outreach) share a common optimal delivery window of September–October. Systematising this as an annual "winter preparation" programme — analogous to the "prepare your home for winter" public awareness campaigns — would have multiplicative benefit. A structured PCN Winter Preparation DES could operationalise this.

## Cross-Cutting Strategy 3: Audit-Feedback as a Universal Improvement Mechanism
Across antimicrobial stewardship, vaccination, inhaler prescribing, and frailty identification, the most reliably effective and low-cost improvement mechanism is providing clinicians with benchmarked, timely feedback on their practice data. The NHS infrastructure (NHSBSA ePACT2, CQRS, NHS RightCare benchmarking, QOF data) already generates this data. The gap is in formatting and delivering it to clinicians in actionable ways at the point of practice.

## Cross-Cutting Strategy 4: Pharmacy as Extended Primary Care Infrastructure
Community pharmacy appears as an enabling intervention across vaccination (P1), antimicrobial stewardship (P2), COPD inhaler optimisation (P7), self-care education (P6), and demand management (P5). The investment in pharmacist training and scope expansion generates compound returns across multiple priorities.

## Cross-Cutting Strategy 5: Patient Activation and Self-Efficacy
Across respiratory self-management (P7), self-care education (P6), frailty support (P8), and mental health (P9), the final common pathway is patient confidence and capability to self-manage. Investing in patient activation tools (Patient Activation Measure [PAM]), health coaching, and shared decision-making training addresses this cross-cutting need.

